---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-caffeine`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Caffeine',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D and Caffeine', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Caffeine: Does Coffee Interfere With Absorption?"
  description="Whether coffee, tea, or caffeine affects vitamin D absorption or bone health — the calcium-caffeine story vs the vitamin-D-caffeine question."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-caffeine", label: "Vitamin D and Caffeine" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Caffeine</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Coffee and caffeine don't meaningfully affect vitamin D absorption. The
      historical concern was about caffeine and calcium — heavy chronic caffeine
      intake modestly increases urinary calcium loss, which matters for bone
      health. Vitamin D itself is fat-soluble and largely independent of caffeine
      intake.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">The calcium-caffeine story</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Caffeine at high chronic doses (&gt; 400 mg/day, 4+ cups of coffee) modestly
      increases urinary calcium excretion — about 4–6 mg extra per 100 mg caffeine.
      Real-world impact on bone density is small in adults with adequate calcium
      intake (~1,000 mg/day). Adding milk to coffee/tea offsets the loss.
      Bone-health concerns primarily affect heavy caffeine users with low
      dietary calcium.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Vitamin D absorption and caffeine</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Vitamin D is a fat-soluble molecule absorbed with dietary fat via bile-
      salt-dependent mixed micelles. Caffeine doesn't participate in this pathway
      and doesn't reduce vitamin D absorption. Taking a vitamin D capsule with
      or near coffee has no meaningful effect on 25(OH)D response.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">One older-adult concern</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      One older study (Rapuri 2007) reported that older women with a specific
      vitamin D receptor (VDR) polymorphism had modestly accelerated bone loss
      with high caffeine intake. Later research has not consistently replicated
      this. The overall bone-density impact of habitual coffee drinking in adults
      is small.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical guide</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Take your vitamin D supplement with your usual meal — coffee timing doesn't matter.</li>
      <li>If you drink &gt; 4 cups coffee/day, ensure adequate calcium (~1,000 mg/day) from food or supplement.</li>
      <li>Milk in coffee provides useful calcium and vitamin D (fortified milk).</li>
      <li>Bone-density concerns are age and sex specific — DEXA over 65, earlier if postmenopausal with risk factors.</li>
    </ol>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> The coffee-vitamin-D discussion is
      largely a non-issue for most adults. Focus energy on the interventions
      that actually matter for bone health.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-caffeine" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
